# -g and the DWARF that comes out of an MLIR pipeline.
#
# Nothing in the front end builds debug metadata. The locations MLIRGen attached
# to each operation survive inlining, shape inference and both lowerings on
# their own, and all that is missing at the end is the scope structure DWARF
# needs -- a compile unit and a subprogram for those locations to hang from.
# Contrast 06_llvm_tutorial, where a DIBuilder is driven by hand during code
# generation.
#
# What -g changes is *whose* compile unit it is. Without it the pipeline runs
# upstream's DIScopeForLLVMFuncOpPass, which invents a compile unit whose
# producer is "MLIR" -- keeping this repo byte-identical to toyc-ch7 when -g is
# absent. With it, src/DebugInfo.cpp attaches a compile unit that names this
# compiler instead. The line table itself is present either way, which is why
# the interesting assertion here is the producer rather than the section.
#
# The limit of all this is worth knowing: line tables only. No DILocalVariable
# is ever created, so a debugger can step by line but cannot print a variable.
#
# REQUIRES: readelf
# RUN: %toyc %s -c -g -o %t.o
# RUN: %readelf --debug-dump=decodedline %t.o | %filecheck %s
#
# RUN: %toyc %s -emit=llvm -g 2>&1 | %filecheck %s --check-prefix=PRODUCER
# RUN: %toyc %s -emit=llvm 2>&1 | %filecheck %s --check-prefix=NOPRODUCER

def main() {
  var a<2, 2> = [[1, 2], [3, 4]];
  print(transpose(a));
}

# The table maps addresses back to lines of this file, not to generated IR.
# CHECK: Contents of the .debug_line section
# CHECK: CU: {{.*}}debug-line-table.toy
# CHECK: File name
# CHECK: debug-line-table.toy

# PRODUCER: producer: "toyc (toy-mlir)"

# Without -g the metadata is upstream's, byte for byte.
# NOPRODUCER: producer: "MLIR"
